    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-4553",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=2x$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{u}^{3}-u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)\\cdot \\sqrt{(2x)^{3}-(2x)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${f}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2\\sqrt{8(2)^{3}-2(2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{64-4} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
